{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:inside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:489edb5ca23bc753b7c5e34394bd99b0a536f3b0879e1bff7d90a3195c5b4406","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.05},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"DECOMPOSITION: real yield = r* + real term premium - repression wedge. Measured today, G7 GDP-weighted (US 0.52, Japan 0.12, Germany 0.12, UK 0.08, France 0.08, Italy 0.06, Canada 0.05): 0.52*2.60 + 0.12*0.60 + 0.12*1.00 + 0.08*1.50 + 0.08*1.30 + 0.06*1.90 + 0.05*1.60 = 1.96%. The US 2.60% TIPS lock is NOT the G7 number; the G7 aggregate is ~0.65pp below it, and most of the 2032-36 change is Japanese and euro-area convergence UP, not US mean reversion.\nARROW DIAGRAM (sign, magnitude on r* or TP in bp, 2036 vs 2025):\n[65+ share +9pp] --(-, desired-wealth/longer-retirement-horizon channel, Auclert-Malmberg-Martenet-Rognlie direction)--> [r*: -20]\n[65+ share +9pp] --(+, life-cycle decumulation flow)--> [r*: +35]  // 65+ hold ~40% of OECD net worth, net worth/GDP ~4.5x, so 1.8x GDP drawn down at ~1.5%/yr = -2.7% of GDP saving flow, offset by the 45-64 cohort's +2.2% of GDP: net desired saving -0.5 to -1.0% of GDP\n[public debt/GDP +15pp G7-weighted] --(+, supply)--> [TP: +38]\n[convenience-yield erosion + levered basis-trade as fragile marginal buyer] --(+)--> [TP: +25, and +60 conditional on stress]\n[AI/grid physical capex, +1.0-1.5pp of GDP investment demand 2027-2031] --(+)--> [r*: +40 in 2027-31, then -25 in 2032-36 if AI-Q4 resolves YES] --> net 2032-36: [r*: -10]\n[cognitive-labour compensation compression] --(+, saving-of-the-rich, MPS gap 0.25)--> [r*: -9]\n[precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty] --(+)--> [r*: -15]\n[China pension subsidy 10.1%->15%+ of fiscal expenditure] --(-, national saving -1.5 to -2pp of China GDP; China ~19% of world)--> [world saving -0.35pp of world GDP] --> [r*: +12]\n[Debt-at-Risk P95 117% of GDP, +2.4pp on a 20% AI equity shock] --(trigger)--> [repression: YCC / captive bank+insurer demand / duration shortening] --(-)--> [wedge: -45 to -80bp when active]\nELASTICITIES I AM RELYING ON (three named):\n(1) d(long real yield)/d(debt/GDP) = +2.5bp per pp. Justification: Laubach (2009) estimates 3-4bp per pp of projected debt; I haircut to 2.5 because a meaningful share of the G7 increment is absorbed by regulatory-captive domestic buyers whose demand is price-inelastic. +15pp G7-weighted -> +38bp, booked to TERM PREMIUM not r*, which is exactly the Section 2A distinction: aging worsens (r-g) by ~+1.0pp through the fiscal-arithmetic and term-premium channels EVEN IF frictionless r* eases.\n(2) d(r*)/d(desired saving) = -35bp per 1pp of GDP. Justification: standard loanable-funds/IS slope used in the Rachel-Summers accounting; implies the -0.5 to -1.0pp of GDP net demographic saving flow is worth +18 to +35bp on r*.\n(3) saving-rate semi-elasticity to income concentration = +0.25pp of GDP per 1pp of income shifted from labour to capital/top. Justification: Mian-Straub-Sufi saving-glut-of-the-rich MPS differential ~0.25-0.35. AI-Q5's 3.5pp compensation-share fall over J/K/M (~25% of OECD GVA) = ~0.9pp of GVA shifted -> +0.22pp of GDP saving -> -8 to -9bp on r*.\nBUILD: G7 r* 2032-36 = 0.95% (today ~0.90%; +35 dissaving, -20 desired wealth, +12 China fiscal, -9 compression, -15 precautionary, -10 net capex cycle = +(-7)bp, essentially flat, with Japan's own r* rising as the BoJ exits). G7 real term premium 2032-36 = 1.35% (today ~1.05%; +38 supply, +25 convenience/plumbing, -30 from a structurally lower-growth, lower-inflation-volatility China anchor on global duration demand... net +33bp). Expected repression wedge = -0.35% (P(explicit or de facto repression 